User‑Defined Data Types (UDD) and Structure Padding in C

1.  Introduction
    C provides four ways to create new data types:
      • struct  – group variables of different types under one name.
      • union   – share the same memory location for multiple members.
      • enum    – define a set of named integer constants.
      • typedef – give a new name (alias) to an existing type.

    This document explains each of them and then focuses on the memory layout
    of structures, including padding and alignment.  Finally, it analyses the
    provided code and shows its output.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
2.  Structures (struct)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    A structure is a collection of members (variables) of possibly different
    types.  Each member has its own storage.

    Syntax:
        struct TagName {
            type1 member1;
            type2 member2;
            …
        };

    Example – The given STUDENT structure:
        typedef struct Student {
            short int    SNo;          // 2 bytes
            char         name[25];     // 25 bytes (each char 1 byte)
            char         dept[4];      // 4 bytes
            char         section;      // 1 byte
            long long    regno;        // 8 bytes
            int          marks[6];     // 24 bytes (6 * 4)
            float        percentage;   // 4 bytes
            char         result;       // 1 byte
        } STUDENT;

    When we declare an array of such structures, the compiler may insert
    padding bytes between members or at the end to satisfy alignment
    requirements.  This is called **structure padding**.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
3.  Unions (union)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    A union stores all its members in the same memory location.  The size of
    a union is the size of its largest member.  Only one member can hold a
    meaningful value at a time.

    Example:
        union Data {
            int   i;
            float f;
            char  str[20];
        };
    Here, sizeof(union Data) = 20 (on most systems).  Modifying i makes f
    and str become garbage (and vice versa).  Unions are useful for memory
    saving and for interpreting the same bytes in different ways.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
4.  Enumerations (enum)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    An enumeration defines a set of named integer constants.  By default, the
    first name has value 0, the next 1, and so on.  You can assign explicit
    values.

    Example:
        enum Level { LOW = 10, MEDIUM = 56, HIGH };
    Here, LOW = 10, MEDIUM = 56, HIGH = 57 (automatic increment).

    Enums make code more readable than raw numbers.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
5.  Typedef
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    typedef creates an alias for an existing data type.  It improves
    readability and portability.

    Example:
        typedef unsigned long ulong;
        ulong distance;   // same as “unsigned long distance;”

    In the given code, the Student struct is typedef’ed to STUDENT, so we can
    write STUDENT data[5]; instead of struct Student data[5];.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
6.  Structure Padding and Alignment
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Why padding exists:
      Processors access memory more efficiently when multi‑byte data is
      aligned at an address that is a multiple of its size.  For example,
      a 4‑byte int should be placed at an address divisible by 4.
      If a member’s offset is not a multiple of its alignment, the compiler
      inserts padding bytes to fix it.  Also, the whole structure may need
      padding at the end so that the overall size is a multiple of the
      largest member’s alignment (so that an array of the structure keeps
      every element aligned).

    Alignment rules (typical for 64‑bit Linux/Windows):
        Type          Size (bytes)   Required alignment
        char          1              1
        short         2              2
        int           4              4
        float         4              4
        long long     8              8
        double        8              8
        pointer       8              8
        array         element size   same as element

    Structure alignment = maximum alignment among its members.
    Structure size is rounded up to a multiple of this alignment.

    Let’s compute the layout of STUDENT on a typical x86‑64 system.
    Assume no special packing (#pragma pack(1) is not used).

    Member          Size   Alignment   Offset (without padding)   Padding added?
    –––––––––––––   ––––   –––––––––   ––––––––––––––––––––––––   –––––––––––––
    SNo (short)     2      2           0                           no
    name[25]        25     1           2                           no
    dept[4]         4      1           27                          no
    section (char)  1      1           31                          no
    Now total offset = 32.  Next member regno (long long) needs
    alignment 8.  Current offset 32 is already a multiple of 8,
    so no padding needed before regno.
    regno (long long)  8   8           32                          no
    marks[6] (int)     24  4           40 (multiple of 4)          no
    percentage (float) 4   4           64                          no
    result (char)      1   1           68                          no
    Total size so far = 69.  The largest alignment in the struct is 8
    (from regno).  The structure size must be a multiple of 8.
    69 → next multiple of 8 is 72.  So 3 bytes of end padding.
    Final sizeof(STUDENT) = 72 bytes.

    (On some compilers/ABIs, arrays might have different alignment; 
     but char arrays have alignment 1, int arrays have alignment 4.
     So the above is correct.)

    The confusing comments in the code (“// 8”, “// 8 8 8”, etc.) were
    perhaps intended to show the alignment boundaries or were just markers.
    Do not rely on them – the compiler does the exact calculation.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
7.  Analysis of the Provided Code
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    The code:

        #include<stdio.h>
        typedef struct Student
        {
            short int SNo;       // 2 bytes
            char name[25];       // 25 bytes
            char dept[4];        // 4 bytes
            char section;        // 1 byte
            long long int regno; // 8 bytes
            int marks[6];        // 24 bytes
            float percentage;    // 4 bytes
            char result;         // 1 byte
        }STUDENT;

        int main()
        {
            STUDENT data[5] = {
                { 1 , "JAYASRI"    , "ECE" , 'A' , 1234567890 , {87,36,45,98,62,34} , 90.00 , 'P'},
                { 2 , "MANEESHA"   , "ECE" , 'A' , 6543212345 , {98,27,35,94,87,39} , 88.00 , 'P'},
                { 3 , "CHANDU"     , "ECE" , 'B' , 9872634234 , {38,47,63,49,58,70} , 91.00 , 'P'},
                { 4 , "KARTHIKEYA" , "ECE" , 'B' , 9862345982 , {34,95,72,93,87,49} , 87.00 , 'P'},
                { 5 , "AJAY KUMAR" , "ECE" , 'B' , 4276543765 , {87,62,38,76,32,87} , 92.00 , 'P'}
            };

            printf("%.5s\n", &data[4].name[5]);
            printf("%.5s\n", (((*(data+4)).name)+5));
            return 0;
        }

    Output:
        KUMAR
        KUMAR

    Why?
        • data[4] is the fifth student (index 4) : “AJAY KUMAR”.
        • name[5] is the character at index 5 of that name.
          Index:  0='A',1='J',2='A',3='Y',4=' ',5='K',6='U',7='M',8='A',9='R'.
          So name[5] points to 'K'.
        • The format specifier “%.5s” tells printf to print at most 5
          characters starting from that address.  The five characters are
          'K', 'U', 'M', 'A', 'R' – forming “KUMAR”.  No null terminator is
          required because the precision limits the output.
        • The second printf uses pointer arithmetic:
          (data+4) gives the address of data[4].
          (*(data+4)).name is data[4].name, i.e., base address of the name array.
          +5 moves the pointer to name[5].
          The result is exactly the same as &data[4].name[5].
          Hence the same output is printed.

    Both statements demonstrate that array indexing and pointer addition are
    interchangeable in C.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
8.  Summary
    • struct   – groups data, may contain padding.
    • union    – overlaps members, saves memory.
    • enum     – named constants.
    • typedef  – creates type aliases.
    • Padding is added automatically by the compiler to satisfy alignment
      requirements and can be influenced by #pragma pack or __attribute__
      if needed.
    • The provided code shows how pointer arithmetic works with structures
      and arrays, and it prints “KUMAR” as a substring of the stored name.

User‑Defined Data Types – Sample Codes

This document provides simple, working examples for struct, union, enum, and typedef.
Each code is self‑contained and can be compiled directly.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1.  struct – grouping related data
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    #include <stdio.h>
    #include <string.h>

    // Define a structure for a Student
    struct Student {
        int roll;
        char name[30];
        float marks;
    };

    int main() {
        // Declare and initialize a structure variable
        struct Student s1 = {101, "Ravi", 85.5};

        // Access members using dot (.) operator
        printf("Roll: %d\n", s1.roll);
        printf("Name: %s\n", s1.name);
        printf("Marks: %.2f\n", s1.marks);

        // Modify a member
        s1.marks = 92.0;
        printf("Updated marks: %.2f\n", s1.marks);

        // Array of structures
        struct Student class[2] = {
            {102, "Priya", 78.0},
            {103, "Amit", 88.5}
        };
        for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++)
            printf("%s scored %.2f\n", class[i].name, class[i].marks);

        return 0;
    }

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
2.  union – sharing memory
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    #include <stdio.h>

    union Data {
        int i;
        float f;
        char str[20];
    };

    int main() {
        union Data d;

        d.i = 100;
        printf("d.i = %d\n", d.i);

        d.f = 3.14;          // overwrites i
        printf("d.f = %.2f\n", d.f);
        printf("d.i now = %d (garbage)\n", d.i);   // unpredictable

        // Size of union = size of largest member
        printf("Size of union = %lu bytes\n", sizeof(d));

        return 0;
    }

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
3.  enum – named integer constants
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    #include <stdio.h>

    enum Day { SUN, MON, TUE, WED, THU, FRI, SAT };

    int main() {
        enum Day today = WED;
        printf("Day number = %d\n", today);   // prints 3

        // Enum with custom values
        enum Status { LOW = 10, MEDIUM = 25, HIGH = 50 };
        enum Status s = MEDIUM;
        printf("Status = %d\n", s);           // prints 25

        // Using enum in switch
        switch (today) {
            case MON: printf("Monday\n"); break;
            case WED: printf("Wednesday\n"); break;
            default:  printf("Other day\n");
        }

        return 0;
    }

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
4.  typedef – creating a type alias
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    #include <stdio.h>

    typedef unsigned long ulong;
    typedef struct {
        int x;
        int y;
    } Point;

    int main() {
        ulong bigNumber = 1234567890;
        printf("Big number: %lu\n", bigNumber);

        Point p1 = {10, 20};
        printf("Point: (%d, %d)\n", p1.x, p1.y);

        return 0;
    }

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
5.  Structure Padding – observing memory size
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    #include <stdio.h>

    struct Example {
        char a;      // 1 byte
        int b;       // 4 bytes
        char c;      // 1 byte
    };

    int main() {
        struct Example e;
        // Expected sum = 1+4+1 = 6, but sizeof shows larger due to padding
        printf("Size of struct = %lu bytes\n", sizeof(e));
        // Typically 12 bytes (1 + 3 padding + 4 + 1 + 3 padding)
        return 0;
    }

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
6.  Nested structures – struct inside struct
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    #include <stdio.h>

    struct Date {
        int day, month, year;
    };

    struct Employee {
        char name[25];
        struct Date joinDate;   // nested structure
        float salary;
    };

    int main() {
        struct Employee emp = {"Meena", {5, 8, 2020}, 45000.0};
        printf("Name: %s\n", emp.name);
        printf("Joined: %d/%d/%d\n", emp.joinDate.day,
               emp.joinDate.month, emp.joinDate.year);
        printf("Salary: %.2f\n", emp.salary);
        return 0;
    }

